## How to test

### 1. Set up the ScrapingBee API key in Doppler

```bash
doppler secrets set SCRAPINGBEE_API_KEY --project pricehop-backend --config dev
# Paste your ScrapingBee API key when prompted
```

### 2. Run unit tests (no API key needed — all mocked)

```bash
bun test
```

### 3. Manual integration test with a real URL

You can write a quick script or use `bun repl`:

```ts
// test-scrape.ts
import { ScrapingBeePageExtractor } from "./src/services/ScrapingBeeService";
import { Effect } from "effect";

const svc = new ScrapingBeePageExtractor(process.env.SCRAPINGBEE_API_KEY!);

// Step 1-2: Extract product data
const extracted = await Effect.runPromise(
  svc.extractProduct("https://www.tiffany.com/jewelry/bracelets/tiffany-t-18k-yellow-gold-bracelets-1366369751.html")
);
console.log("Extracted:", extracted);

// Step 4a: Search for same product in Japan
const query = `${extracted.name} ${extracted.skus ?? ""} japanese price`;
const jpUrl = await Effect.runPromise(
  svc.searchProduct(query, "jp")
);
console.log("Japan URL:", jpUrl);

// Step 4b: Extract from Japan URL
if (jpUrl) {
  const jpData = await Effect.runPromise(svc.extractProduct(jpUrl));
  console.log("Japan data:", jpData);
}
```

Run it:
```bash
doppler run --project pricehop-backend --config dev -- bun test-scrape.ts
```

### 4. End-to-end test (mocked, already included)

The e2e test at `tests/e2e/indexing-flow.test.ts` exercises the full flow — extract source product, search per region, extract target URLs, write prices to Convex — all with `MockScrapingBeeService`. It runs as part of `bun test`.

### 5. Verify BrightData is fully removed

```bash
grep -r "brightdata\|bright.data\|BrightData\|BRIGHT_DATA" src/ convex/ tests/
# Should return nothing
```
